<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cumaean Sibyl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sibyl of Cumae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cumaean Sibyl was the prophetess who presided over the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Cumae, the Greek colony near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Roman tradition said she sold the Sibylline Books to King Tarquin, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> she guides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> through the underworld. A long trapezoidal gallery cut into the rock at Cumae has been identified as her cave.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cumaean-sibyl.jpg" alt="Cumaean Sibyl" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: АНО «Международный нумизматический клуб» · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INC-1813-r_%D0%90%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%83%D1%81_%D0%BE%D0%BA._43_%D0%B3._%D0%B4%D0%BE_%D0%BD._%D1%8D._%D0%9C%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%9E%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B8%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%9B%D1%8E%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A6%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B8_%D0%93%D0%B0%D0%B9_%D0%9D%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BD_(%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%81).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
